  "content": {
    "id": "p62-q1-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "1",
    "prompt": "$P(x)=x^3-7x+6$. Show that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $P(x)$ and hence, by using long division, express $P(x)$ as a product of linear factors.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$P(1)=0$; $P(x)=(x-1)(x-2)(x+3)$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}P(1)&=1^3-7(1)+6\\\\P(1)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nBy the factor theorem, $(x-1)$ is a factor of $P(x)$. Insert the missing $x^2$ term with coefficient zero and divide:\n$$\\begin{array}{rrrrr}&x^2&+x&-6&\\\\\\hline x-1&x^3&+0x^2&-7x&+6\\\\&x^3&-x^2&&\\\\\\hline &&x^2&-7x&+6\\\\&&x^2&-x&\\\\\\hline &&&-6x&+6\\\\&&&-6x&+6\\\\\\hline &&&&0\\end{array}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}P(x)&=(x-1)(x^2+x-6)\\\\&=(x-1)(x-2)(x+3)\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
